WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 69: The Day Ann Hodges Was Struck by a Meteorite

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:15.520
[Rebecca] On a clear mild afternoon in late November 1954, an eight and a half-pound blackened stone fell from outer space at thousands of miles an hour.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:31.840
[Rebecca] After blazing through the Earth's atmosphere over three southeastern states, the meteorite crashed through the wooden roof of a modest rented farmhouse in Silicauga, Alabama, ricocheted off a heavy console radio, and struck 34-year-old Ann Hodges as she napped on her living room sofa.

5
00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:39.680
[Rebecca] Anne Hodges became the only documented human in recorded scientific history confirmed to have been directly struck by a space rock.

6
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.560
[Rebecca] But the physical impact was only the beginning.

7
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:50.800
[Rebecca] What followed was an extraordinary battle of property rights, military confiscation, media hysteria, and psychological trauma.

8
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:58.000
[Rebecca] George brings us the surreal, cosmic saga of the Siliconga meteorite.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:11.840
[George] On Tuesday afternoon, November 30th, 1954, at approximately 12:46 in the afternoon, thousands of people across Alabama, Georgia, and Mississippi witnessed a terrifying cosmic spectacle.

10
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:23.680
[George] Streaking across the azure autumn sky was a blinding fireball surrounded by a reddish smoke trail, described by witnesses as resembling a giant Roman candle or an exploding aircraft.

11
00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:34.560
[George] The object was an ancient stony chondrite meteorite, roughly the size of a bowling ball, traveling at an estimated 10,000 miles an hour as it entered the upper atmosphere.

12
00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:50.240
[George] The friction of the air superheated the meteorite's surface to thousands of degrees, creating a fiery friction mantle before the object detonated into several fragments with three concussive sonic booms that rattled windows and shook ground foundations for 50 miles around.

13
00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.320
[George] In Montgomery, citizens called civil defense stations fearing a Soviet nuclear missile strike.

14
00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:02.840
[George] In Birmingham, air traffic controllers scrambled to verify whether a commercial airliner had exploded in midair.

15
00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:15.160
[George] Witnesses in three states described the blinding fireball as brighter than the midday sun, followed by a series of sonic booms that shook farmhouse foundations and set farm animals running in panic.

16
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:27.000
[George] The dramatic events unfolded against a backdrop of deep cultural transformation, as rural communities confronted the rapid onset of modern industrial technology and national media attention.

17
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:39.160
[George] Anne Hodges was enjoying a quiet afternoon resting on her living room sofa under warm quilts, recovering from a minor illness while her mother worked in the adjacent kitchen of their rented wood frame farmhouse.

18
00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:50.520
[George] Meanwhile, in the small rural community of Oak Grove near Silicauga, Alabama, 34-year-old Anne Elizabeth Hodges was resting inside her rented white frame farmhouse.

19
00:02:50.520 --> 00:03:00.600
[George] Anne was feeling unwell and had curled up under two heavy quilts on the living room sofa for an afternoon nap, while her mother, Ida Franklin, sat sewing in the next room.

20
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:04.360
[George] At 12.46, disaster crashed from the heavens.

21
00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:14.520
[George] An eight and a half-pound blackened fragment of the meteorite, measuring eight inches long and four inches wide, tore straight through the wooden heartpine roof of the cottage.

22
00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:27.240
[George] It smashed through the ceiling lath and plaster in a shower of white dust, bounced violently off the heavy wooden cabinet of a Philco console radio, and struck Anne Hodges squarely on her left hip and abdomen.

23
00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:33.800
[George] Anne bolted awake in terror, screaming that the chimney had exploded or that a bomb had struck the home.

24
00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.240
[George] The room was thick with gray smoke and choking dust.

25
00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:46.640
[George] Lying on the linoleum floor near the sofa was an egg-shaped, charcoal-black rock the size of a grapefruit, still radiating heat from its journey through space.

26
00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:58.560
[George] The eight-pound space rock had travelled across hundreds of millions of miles of interplanetary space before intersecting Earth orbit, crashing through the pine roof of the modest Alabama farmhouse.

27
00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:09.520
[George] Eyewitness accounts recorded in local diaries and regional newspapers, captured the tension and uncertainty of the moment, preserving vivid details for future generations of historians.

28
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:23.360
[George] The fiery meteor streaked across the clear afternoon sky over three southern states, generating a series of sonic booms that rattled windowpanes and caused local farmers to believe a military plane had crashed in the pine woods.

29
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:25.520
[George] Anne was rushed to local physician Dr.

30
00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:26.400
[George] Moody C.

31
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.200
[George] Fowler.

32
00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:27.760
[George] When Dr.

33
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.480
[George] Fowler examined her, he was astonished.

34
00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:38.560
[George] Beneath her quilts, the heavy wool blankets had absorbed the brunt of the kinetic impact, preventing internal hemorrhaging or broken bones.

35
00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:50.240
[George] However, across Anne's left hip and side was a grotesque, radiating contusion the size of a dinner plate, described by doctors as resembling a giant, purplish-black pineapple.

36
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:58.160
[George] Geologists from the Geological Survey of Alabama examined the rock and confirmed that it was indeed an extraterrestrial meteorite.

37
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:07.920
[George] Within hours, the peaceful front lawn of the Hodges farmhouse was overrun by hundreds of shouting reporters, newsreel cameramen, and curious tourists.

38
00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:16.160
[George] Neighbors trampled through Anne's vegetable garden, peering through windows and trying to pry shingles from the hole in the roof as souvenirs.

39
00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:29.840
[George] Anne, a shy, private countrywoman who suffered from fragile nerves, was overwhelmed by the sudden global notoriety, retreat into her bedroom with ice packs as telegrams poured in from London, Tokyo, and New York.

40
00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:39.640
[George] Life magazine photographers arrived in Silicauga, capturing famous images of Anne pointing to the pineapple-shaped contusion that covered her left hip and side.

41
00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:51.960
[George] Local doctors were astonished to find a bowling ball-sized bruise covering Hodges's left hip, noting that if the eight-pound fragment had struck her head or chest, the impact would have been instantly fatal.

42
00:05:51.960 --> 00:06:02.760
[George] The following morning, Siliconga police chief Bill Lampley confiscated the meteorite, handing it over to intelligence officers from Maxwell Air Force Base in Montgomery.

43
00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:16.360
[George] In 1954, during the height of the Cold War Red Scare, the United States Air Force was intensely paranoid about foreign aerial reconnaissance, advanced Soviet rocketry, and unidentified flying objects.

44
00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:32.520
[George] Air Force scientists spent several days testing the rock in laboratories to verify that it was not a radioactive fragment of a Soviet spacecraft or an atomic weapon component before officially confirming that it was an ancient celestial chondrite composed of nickel iron and silicates.

45
00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:43.960
[George] When the military prepared to keep the space rock as federal property, Anne's husband, Eugene Hodges, a utility company tree trimmer who had been at work during the strike, was furious.

46
00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:51.320
[George] Eugene hired legal counsel, boldly declaring to national reporters I feel like the meteorite is ours.

47
00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.880
[George] It fell on our house and hit my wife.

48
00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:59.480
[George] Uncle Sam might have the Air Force, but he doesn't own every rock that falls out of the sky.

49
00:06:59.480 --> 00:07:10.200
[George] Cold War fears gripped the local community as Air Force officers whisked the rock away for classified testing at Maxwell Air Force Base, fearing it was a Soviet surveillance device.

50
00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:11.400
[George] Police Chief H.

51
00:07:11.400 --> 00:07:11.640
[George] T.

52
00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:23.680
[George] Brooks secured the mysterious black stone in the city vault, while United States Air Force intelligence officers flew in from Maxwell Air Force Base to inspect the object for signs of Soviet rocketry.

53
00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:30.400
[George] The Air Force backed down and returned the meteorite, but an even uglier legal dispute erupted on the home front.

54
00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.360
[George] The Hodges did not own the Oak Grove farmhouse.

55
00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.320
[George] They rented the property from a 51-year-old widowed landlady named Bertie Guy.

56
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:49.680
[George] Bertie Guy filed a lawsuit against Eugene and Anne Hodges, claiming that as the legal owner of the real estate and the roof through which the meteorite had crashed, the space rock belonged to her.

57
00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:57.840
[George] Birdie argued that under classical English common law, any mineral falling upon real property became part of the landholder's estate.

58
00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:04.080
[George] The Hodges counterargued that the stone was a personal windfall that had directly impacted Anne's flesh.

59
00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:14.160
[George] For over a year, the two women feuded in state courts and traded bitter accusations in the press, while public sympathy across America sided heavily with Anne Hodges.

60
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.520
[George] Meanwhile, a local black tenant farmer named Julius McKinney discovered a second.

61
00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:24.320
[George] Smaller two-pound fragment of the same meteorite in the dirt road near his home.

62
00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:32.400
[George] McKinney quietly sold his rock to a private collector for enough cash to purchase a new home and a truck, avoiding all controversy.

63
00:08:32.400 --> 00:08:44.480
[George] The nationwide debate over ownership of the Space Rock divided legal scholars, who debated ancient Roman property law, English common law, and modern frontier windfall precedents.

64
00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:56.880
[George] Landlady Bertie Guy filed a formal lawsuit claiming ownership of the Space Rock, arguing that because the meteorite had penetrated the roof of her property, the valuable stone belonged exclusively to the homeowner.

65
00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:06.360
[George] In late 1955, exhausted by mounting legal expenses and deteriorating public relations, Bertie Guy settled the lawsuit out of court.

66
00:09:06.360 --> 00:09:15.720
[George] In exchange for $500, she relinquished all legal claims to the meteorite, and Eugene and Anne Hodges took full ownership of the stone.

67
00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:25.560
[George] Eugene believed their fortune was made, expecting a wealthy private museum or university to pay thousands of dollars for the world's most famous space artifact.

68
00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:35.720
[George] But by 1956, the media whirlwind had moved on to other sensations, and the highest private offer the Hodges received was an insulting few hundred dollars.

69
00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:45.960
[George] Feeling that the meteorite had brought nothing but misery, legal bills, and public intrusion into their quiet lives, Anne insisted that the stone be removed from their home.

70
00:09:45.960 --> 00:10:01.720
[George] In 1956, Anne Hodges donated the four billion-year-old space rock to the Alabama Museum of Natural History at the University of Alabama in Tuscaloosa, where it was placed on permanent public exhibition inside a secure velvet-lined glass case.

71
00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:14.200
[George] The four billion-year-old chondrite was safely preserved in the Alabama Museum of Natural History, where scientists and school children marvel at the only rock from outer space ever confirmed to strike a human.

72
00:10:14.200 --> 00:10:26.280
[George] The courage and resilience demonstrated by everyday citizens during this critical trial inspired observers across the country, showing that ordinary people can rise to meet extraordinary challenges.

73
00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:37.000
[George] The unrelenting media circus and legal wrangling took a severe psychological toll on Anne Hodges, who struggled with nervous exhaustion and health problems for the remainder of her life.

74
00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:43.240
[George] Tragically, Anne Hodges never truly recovered from the psychological aftermath of the meteorite strike.

75
00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:50.480
[George] The intense media scrutiny, the invasive crowds, and the prolonged legal war shattered her fragile emotional health.

76
00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:58.960
[George] She suffered from recurring panic attacks, chronic depression, and social anxiety, convinced that another rock might fall from the sky.

77
00:10:58.960 --> 00:11:06.320
[George] In 1964, the emotional strain caused Anne and Eugene Hodges to divorce after 16 years of marriage.

78
00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:18.720
[George] In September 1972, at the age of just 52, Anne Hodges passed away from kidney failure in a Siliconga nursing home, laid to rest in the quiet soil of Childersburg Cemetery.

79
00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:29.200
[George] Today, the Silicauga meteorite, officially catalogued by scientists as the Hodges Meteorite, remains one of the most celebrated specimens in planetary science.

80
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:40.320
[George] It stands as a reminder of our fragile place in a vast, dynamic cosmos, and the remarkable, melancholy story of an ordinary Alabama woman who was touched by a falling star.

81
00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:51.760
[George] Anne Hodges' quiet resting place in Childersburg Cemetery stands as a solemn monument to an ordinary woman whose life was touched by the boundless, unpredictable mystery of the cosmos.

82
00:11:51.760 --> 00:12:03.520
[George] Today, historic markers and museum collections honor these legendary figures, reminding us that the spirit of American adventure and perseverance continues to guide our shared journey forward.

83
00:12:03.520 --> 00:12:16.560
[George] Today, the Hodges Meteorite is proudly exhibited at the Alabama Museum of Natural History in Tuscaloosa, remaining the only verified celestial object in human history to have struck a living human being.

84
00:12:17.080 --> 00:12:25.160
[Rebecca] The story of Ann Hodges reminds us of the strange, unpredictable intersections between human lives and the infinite cosmos.

85
00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:26.520
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

86
00:12:26.480 --> 00:12:33.800
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey to Mississippi for the devastating deluge that changed American music and politics.

87
00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:36.040
[Rebecca] The Great Flood of 1927.

